MIOpen (pinned tag rocm-6.4.4, commit 4d17e9a): make ConvBinWinogradRxS and
ConvBinWinograd3x3U not applicable on gfx803.

WHY
---
On gfx803 these two solvers are MIOpen's first choice for nearly every fp32
3x3 convolution (forward and backward-data), and both run legacy hand-written
"wheel" assembly kernels (conv_3x3_wheel_alpha_v9_0_15_gfx8.inc for RxS,
conv_3x3_wheel_alpha_v3_0b.inc for 3x3U) launched persistently, one 512-thread
workgroup per CU, each holding all 64 KiB of LDS. On an RX 590 they do two
things wrong.

They corrupt memory. Memory one of these kernels wrote reverts to that
kernel's output after later kernels overwrite it, once PyTorch's caching
allocator hands the block to a new tensor. In an SD1.5 VAE encode, the
tensors computed right after the encoder (logvar, then 0.5 * logvar, then
std) land in memory the final 3x3 conv wrote; every wrong logvar element
equals the encoder output's old value at the same address (333/333, 335/335
and 328/328 in three runs), and keeping the encoder output alive makes every
run correct. sd-scripts' latent caching samples from that std, so 7 to 11 of
34 cached latents came out with std from 80 to 5e16 instead of about 4.5,
and LoRA training loss sat at 0.6-1.2 instead of 0.1-0.2. A full device
synchronize does not help, writes into never-used memory nearly never go
wrong, and neither bounding the kernel's output buffer descriptor by the
real output size nor marking its stores glc changes anything.

They also hang the GPU. In every decoded GPU hang under sustained load, the
stuck dispatch was one of these kernels (512x1x1 workgroups, grid
512 * CU count, 64 KiB LDS; identical code bytes in a training hang and in a
standalone replay). An uncapped replay of one SD1.5 UNet block crashed the
host 5 times out of 5 within 3.5 to 21 minutes; with these solvers out of
the way the same replay ran 45 minutes clean, and a full 1200-step LoRA
training run ran 2 hours 34 minutes clean.

Disabling only RxS is not enough: MIOpen then selects 3x3U, which is built the
same way and corrupts the same way. The only other solvers MIOpen can use for
these convolutions on gfx803 are its GEMM-based ones (GemmFwdRest,
GemmBwdRest and the 1x1 GEMM variants); every other Winograd solver is gfx9+
only or weight-gradient only. The GEMM solvers are correct: the cached
latents match a CPU encode exactly, and a full-UNet GPU-vs-CPU check passes.
They are slower than the Winograd kernels per convolution; with this repo's
gfx803 SGEMM shim (patches/sgemm-shim-batched), training runs at the same
speed as before (8.3 s/step against 8.1-8.4).

To reproduce without this patch, with PyTorch on gfx803: encode one image at
512x512 with the SD1.5 VAE in float32 the way sd-scripts caches latents
(vae.encode(x).latent_dist), several times, and compare latent_dist.std with
a CPU encode; most runs show elements wrong by orders of magnitude.
MIOPEN_DEBUG_CONV_WINOGRAD=0 makes every run match, and so does this patch: on
an RX 590, with a find-db that lists ConvBinWinogradRxS first and no
environment override, MIOpen built with it selects GemmFwdRest/GemmFwd1x1_0_1
instead, and the cached latents match the CPU in 3/3 runs.

WHAT
----
Return false from both solvers' IsApplicable on gfx803, right after the device
name is read. Every other device keeps both solvers.

--- a/src/solver/conv/conv_bin_winoRxS.cpp
+++ b/src/solver/conv/conv_bin_winoRxS.cpp
@@ -254,6 +254,13 @@
         return false;
 
     const auto name = ctx.GetStream().GetDeviceName();
+    // WINOGRAD_BIN_GFX803_DISABLE_PATCH: on gfx803 this solver's kernel corrupts
+    // memory the convolution wrote: once that memory is reused, it reverts to the
+    // kernel's output after later kernels overwrite it, and the same kernel is the
+    // stuck dispatch in GPU hangs under sustained load. The GEMM solvers MIOpen
+    // falls back to are correct. See winograd-bin-gfx803-disable.patch.
+    if(name == "gfx803")
+        return false;
     const bool fp16 = problem.IsFp16();
     if(fp16)
     {
--- a/src/solver/conv/conv_bin_wino3x3U.cpp
+++ b/src/solver/conv/conv_bin_wino3x3U.cpp
@@ -60,6 +60,13 @@
         return false;
 
     const auto name = ctx.GetStream().GetDeviceName();
+    // WINOGRAD_BIN_GFX803_DISABLE_PATCH: on gfx803 this solver's kernel corrupts
+    // memory the convolution wrote: once that memory is reused, it reverts to the
+    // kernel's output after later kernels overwrite it, and the same kernel is the
+    // stuck dispatch in GPU hangs under sustained load. The GEMM solvers MIOpen
+    // falls back to are correct. See winograd-bin-gfx803-disable.patch.
+    if(name == "gfx803")
+        return false;
     if(!(name == "gfx803" || name == "gfx900" || name == "gfx906" || name == "gfx908"))
         return false;
 
